          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 30-year-old pilgrim is brought in with profuse sweating, pallor, nausea, and a</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old pilgrim is brought in with profuse sweating, pallor, nausea, and a core temperature of 38.5°C. He is conscious but weak. Which heat illness does this presentation most likely represent?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Heat cramps</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Heat cramps present with isolated muscle cramping from sodium depletion, without systemic signs like pallor, nausea, or raised core temperature.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Heat exhaustion</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Heat exhaustion is caused by fluid loss from prolonged sweating. Preserved sweating, consciousness, and a core temperature below 40°C distinguish it from heat stroke.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Heat stroke</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In heat stroke, core temperature exceeds 40°C, sweating stops (anhidrosis), and the patient develops confusion, seizures, or coma — none of which are present here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sun stroke</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sun stroke is heat stroke triggered specifically by direct sun exposure. With preserved consciousness and active sweating, heat stroke of any type is ruled out.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the heat illness spectrum. The key discriminators are core temperature (above or below 40°C), sweating status (present versus absent), and level of consciousness."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Heat exhaustion is characterised by dehydration, profuse sweating, pallor, and weakness with core temperature below 40°C — distinguishing it from the thermoregulatory failure of heat stroke."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
