          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During post-mortem examination of a homeless man found dead in winter with his c</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During post-mortem examination of a homeless man found dead in winter with his clothing removed, which finding in the stomach would most strongly support a diagnosis of hypothermia?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Curling's ulcers</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Curling's ulcers are acute stress ulcers seen after severe burns, not cold exposure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mallory-Weiss tears</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Mallory-Weiss tears result from forceful vomiting or retching and are not specific to hypothermia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Wischnewski spots</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Wischnewski spots — reddish-brown erosions of the gastric mucosa — are the hallmark post-mortem finding in hypothermia. The removed clothing is paradoxical undressing, caused by a false sensation of warmth in severe cold exposure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dieulafoy's lesion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dieulafoy's lesion is a vascular malformation causing upper GI bleeding, unrelated to hypothermia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In hypothermia, look for Wischnewski spots — reddish-brown erosions in the gastric mucosa. The removed clothing likely represents paradoxical undressing (a false sensation of warmth), not assault."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Wischnewski spots in the stomach combined with paradoxical undressing at the scene are two findings that together strongly support a diagnosis of hypothermia death."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
