          "label": "<p>Which skin finding is pathognomonic for a lightning strike and absent in domesti</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which skin finding is pathognomonic for a lightning strike and absent in domestic electrocution?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Joule burn with a pale punched-out centre</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The Joule burn (electric mark) with a pale centre from coagulation necrosis is the hallmark of domestic or industrial electrocution, not lightning.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lichtenberg figure</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Lichtenberg figures are red, fern-like branching patterns on the skin produced by the flashover effect of lightning's brief, massive discharge. They are pathognomonic for lightning and fade within 24–48 hours.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Contact burn at the entry point</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Contact burns can occur in both lightning and electrocution and are therefore not specific to either mechanism.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Charring of skin along the current path</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Charring may occur with any high-energy electrical or thermal injury and is not pathognomonic for lightning.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Lightning produces fern-like branching marks called Lichtenberg figures from surface flashover — pathognomonic and transient. Domestic electrocution instead produces the Joule burn with a pale, punched-out centre."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Lichtenberg figures are unique to lightning because of its surface flashover mechanism. They fade within a day or two, so early photography at the scene is forensically essential."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
