          "label": "<p>A 70-year-old woman living alone is found dead in a locked, unventilated room du</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 70-year-old woman living alone is found dead in a locked, unventilated room during a summer heat wave, with a core temperature of 42°C. Neighbours report no one checked on her for three days. How should the forensic physician approach this death?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Certify as natural death due to age-related thermoregulatory decline</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Although age reduces thermoregulatory capacity, the circumstances — locked unventilated room, no care for three days — demand investigation beyond a natural-death certification.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Classify as accidental heat stroke from environmental exposure</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Classifying this as purely accidental overlooks the duty-of-care question. A vulnerable person was left without supervision in dangerous conditions.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Document heat stroke findings and flag the case for investigation of possible criminal negligence</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The forensic physician establishes the cause (heat stroke) and documents the circumstances that raise a duty-of-care question. The same agent — heat — leads to a different medicolegal pathway when the victim is vulnerable and care was withheld.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Conclude homicide by deliberate heat exposure</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Homicide requires evidence of deliberate intent to cause death. The appropriate initial approach is to flag possible negligence for the investigating authority, not to presume homicide.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Context determines the medicolegal pathway. A heat death in a vulnerable person left without care in a locked room raises the question of criminal negligence — the forensic physician must document findings and flag the duty-of-care breach for investigation."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The forensic physician establishes the cause of death and flags the context for the investigating authority. The same physical agent produces different legal outcomes — heat stroke in a neglected elderly person raises criminal negligence, not just accidental death."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
